{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Report (from a rebar client)\nA Rebar CLI quirk treated `create task --help` as a literal task and created an accidental placeholder ticket.\n\n## Observed\nRunning `rebar create task --help` (an attempt to view help for the create-task command) does NOT print help — instead the parser consumes `--help` as the positional title argument, so a ticket titled `--help` (a placeholder) is actually CREATED.\n\n## Expected\n`create task --help` (and `--help`/`-h` on any create subcommand) should print the command's usage/help and create NOTHING. Help must never be interpreted as ticket content.\n\n## Impact\nAccidental placeholder tickets pollute the store and require cleanup; the write side-effect on a help request is surprising and violates least-astonishment.\n\n## Notes / possible cause\nThe create command path likely reads the first positional as the title before any --help interception. Compare with the argparse standardization landed for the write commands (claim/transition, ticket fd48-7a80-33e9-4400): --help there is served byte-exact by the top-level dispatcher and never reaches the handler. The create path may bypass that dispatcher help interception, or accept a bare positional that swallows `--help`. Needs repro + root-cause.\n\n## Repro\n1. `rebar create task --help`\n2. Observe: no help text; a new ticket titled `--help` is created (verify via `rebar list`).\n\n## Confirmed root cause\n`rebar._cli.main` only treats `--help`/`-h` as a help request when it is the FIRST\ntoken after the subcommand (`rest[0]`): the guard is\n`if rest and rest[0] in (\"--help\", \"-h\") and sub not in _HIDDEN_ALIASES:`. For\n`create task --help`, `rest == [\"task\", \"--help\"]`, so `rest[0] == \"task\"` and the guard\nis skipped; dispatch falls through to `composer.create_cli`, whose hand-rolled parser\nreads `rest[0]` as the ticket type and `rest[1]` (`--help`) as the title, then performs\nthe CREATE write. The commands that \"own\" their `--help` (review-plan, workflow, audit, …)\nare intercepted earlier and are unaffected; the bug hits the fall-through write commands.\n\n## Fix\nIn `rebar._cli.main`, recognise `--help`/`-h` appearing ANYWHERE in `rest` before a `--`\nterminator (not only at `rest[0]`) as a help request for a routable subcommand, matching\nargparse semantics (help short-circuits regardless of position). A small `_wants_help(rest)`\nhelper scans `rest`, stopping at `--`. `_HIDDEN_ALIASES` stays excluded; the earlier\nown-their-help intercepts are untouched. This serves the byte-exact pinned usage text via\nthe existing `_emit_subcommand_help(sub)` and performs NO write.\n\n## Testing\n- Regression: `main([\"create\", \"task\", \"--help\"])` returns 0, prints the create usage to\n  stdout, and creates NO ticket (assert the store is unchanged / no new alias).\n- Position parity: `-h` in a later position and after other flags is also honoured.\n- Terminator: `--` before `--help` is respected (help NOT triggered past `--`).\n- Unchanged: `rebar create task \"real title\"` still creates; `create --help` (rest[0])\n  still shows help; an unknown subcommand still errors.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `rebar create task --help` prints the create usage (exit 0) and creates NO ticket.\n- [ ] `--help`/`-h` anywhere before a `--` terminator triggers subcommand help for routable\n      write commands (create/edit/comment/link/…), not just at `rest[0]`.\n- [ ] Commands that own their own `--help` and unknown-subcommand handling are unchanged.\n- [ ] A regression test covers `create task --help` (no write, usage shown).\n- [ ] `make lint` + `make typecheck` pass and the CLI help golden/contract tests stay green."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787123990907176001,"uuid":"fc2fab61-7d2a-486e-8e96-61f395589b52"}